<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bhumihar</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Bhumihars are a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> landholding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">caste</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bihar">Bihar</a> and eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uttar-pradesh">Uttar Pradesh</a> in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, who claim <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmin</a> status. Many held large <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zamindar">zamindari</a> estates under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughals</a>, the Nawabs of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/awadh">Awadh</a> and the British. The Rajas of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varanasi">Benares</a> were a Bhumihar family, as were the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharaja">Maharajas</a> of Hathwa and Bettiah, and Bhumihars were prominent in the politics of 20th-century Bihar.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bhumihar.jpg" alt="Bhumihar" width="320" height="252" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: BOURNE &amp; SHEPHERD · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Majaraja_of_Benares_and_Suite,_1870s.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Bhumihars, also called Bhuinhar and Babhan, live mainly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bihar">Bihar</a>, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mithila">Mithila</a>, the Purvanchal region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uttar-pradesh">Uttar Pradesh</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jharkhand">Jharkhand</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bundelkhand">Bundelkhand</a> region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madhya-pradesh">Madhya Pradesh</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a>. The word bhumihar, from bhumi, land, is first recorded in the records of the United Provinces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agra">Agra</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/awadh">Oudh</a> in 1865, and the community adopted the name Bhumihar Brahmin in the late 19th century to stress its claim to priestly rank. Legends variously derive them from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputs">Rajput</a> men and Brahmin women, from Brahman-Buddhists who lost their high position, or from Parashurama; Bhumihars reject the stories of mixed or fallen status and claim to be pure Brahmins.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>By the 16th century Bhumihars controlled large stretches of land in north <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bihar">Bihar</a>, and by the late 18th century they and the Bihari Rajputs were the leading landholders of the region. As Mughal power weakened, small Bhumihar states arose, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varanasi">Benares</a>, Bettia, Tekari, Hathwa and Sheohar. Under Cheyt Singh, Raja of Banaras, Bhumihars revolted against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a> in 1781, after which the Company recruited Bhumihar sepoys in large numbers until 1857. Early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">censuses</a> classed them as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shudra">Shudras</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">caste</a> associations, beginning with a sabha founded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/patna">Patna</a> in 1889, pressed until later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-raj">Raj</a> censuses recognised them as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmins</a>.</p><p>From the 1910s Bhumihars were prominent in peasant, leftist and independence movements. Sahajanand <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saraswati">Saraswati</a>, who led the tenants' faction of the Bhumihar Mahasabha, founded a peasant movement open to all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">castes</a> that grew into the All India Kisan Sabha. Shri Krishna Singh, Premier and later Chief Minister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bihar">Bihar</a>, was the first chief minister in India to abolish the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zamindar">zamindari</a> system. Bhumihar political influence declined after his death in 1961 and further after 1990; some Bhumihars then joined Ranvir Sena, a private militia founded in 1994 that committed atrocities against lower castes, among them the Laxmanpur Bathe massacre.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Bhumihars follow a subset of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmin</a> rituals and call themselves tri-karma Brahmins. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bihar">Bihar</a> many took the surname Sharma and the title Pandit in the 20th century, and Mishra, Chaudhary, Pathak and Upadhyaya are also common, as is Singh. Though often thought of as landlords, most were tenants or middle peasants, and in towns they worked as professionals and bureaucrats but also as factory workers, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a> miners and labourers.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bhumihar” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Most of the great zamindars were of the Hindu high castes, Brahmin, Rajput, <b>Bhumihar</b> or Kayastha, and the British gave loyal ones titles such as Raja, Rai Bahadur, Nawab or Khan Bahadur.</blockquote><a href="/societies/british-raj">Social Hierarchy of British India</a></li><li><blockquote>Mansa Ram, a <b>Bhumihar</b> landholder, obtained the zamindari of Benares around 1737 under the Nawab of Awadh.</blockquote><a href="/state/benares-state">Benares State</a></li></ul></section>
